          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/Hybrid-Search\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two distinct sources now cover this pattern; no direct contradictions.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets\\u2014often via **reciprocal rank fusion**\\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nThis approach consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- **Rare identifiers** (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- **Natural language intent** (semantic and contextual meaning)\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production [[concepts/RAG]] systems.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nUse reciprocal rank fusion for robust retrieval. This technique normalizes rankings from different retrieval methods and merges them into a unified result set, balancing the contributions of each method fairly.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval pattern overview (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-07)\\n\u0022}"
